 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil practice law and rules, in relation to permitting\ngrand jury inspection of medical records pursuant to subpoena duces tecum\npowers",
    "basePrintNo": "S436",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S436-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd R3122, §4504, CPLR",
    "lawSection": "Civil Practice Law and Rules",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S436\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil practice law and rules, in relation to permit-\nting grand jury inspection of medical records pursuant to subpoena duces\ntecum powers\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation would amend the Civil Practice Law and Rules to permit\ngrand jury inspection of medical records pursuant to subpoena duces\ntecum powers.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends Rule 3122(2)(a) of the CPLR to include a subpoena\nissued on behalf of a grand jury.\n\nSection 2: Adds a new subdivision to CPLR Section 4504 to provide that a\nphysician, dentist, podiatrist, chiropractor, nurse, and other entities\nmust disclose information regarding a patient who has been a victim of a\ncrime and directly pertaining to such crime in response to a trial\nsubpoena or a subpoena issued on behalf of a grand jury.\n\nSection 3: Severability clause. Section 4: Effective Date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2011, the Office of Court Administration's Advisory Committee on\nCivil Practice drafted legislation to clarify that trial subpoenas were\nable to pierce the physician-patient privilege. The legislation was\ndrafted in response to a court ruling that became widely followed and\nlimited the authority of the court to order the production of medical\nrecords pursuant to a trial subpoena without the patient's signed\nauthorization. However, when the legislation was drafted, it did not\nextend the same privilege to grand jury subpoenas. This bill extends the\nprivilege and therefore allows the physician-patient privilege to be\npierced by subpoenas issued on behalf of a grand jury. Allowing access\nto medical records from a grand jury subpoena is especially important in\nfamily violence and vehicular crimes cases, as a grand jury decides\nwhether there is enough evidence for a trial. This legislation will\nensure that access and address concerns of medical providers that they\nare not violating the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability\nAct.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: S4759C (Kaminsky) passed Senate.\n\n2019: S4759A (Kaminsky) passed Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the 1st of November next succeeding the\ndate on which it becomes law.\n",
